content:
I have a counter that should tick once a second, but the rendered value gets stuck at 1.

```javascript
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setInterval(() => {
      setCount(count + 1);
    }, 1000);
    return () => clearInterval(id);
  }, []);

  return <p>{count}</p>;
}
```

I expected 1, 2, 3, ... and I get 0, then 1, then nothing. React 19.2 in a plain Vite app; I already removed StrictMode to rule out the double mount, no difference. If I add `count` to the dependency array it does count up, but then the interval is cleared and recreated on every tick and my timestamps drift by 20-40ms each second. Why does the callback keep seeing `count === 0` when the component clearly re-rendered?

I guard the ref before using it, but the compiler still complains inside the timeout.

```typescript
const inputRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null);

const focusLater = () => {
  if (!inputRef.current) return;
  setTimeout(() => {
    inputRef.current.focus();
    //       ~~~~~~~ Object is possibly 'null'. ts(2531)
  }, 100);
};
```

The early return is right there two lines above, so I expected the narrowing to hold. TypeScript 5.9, strict mode on. Moving the check inside the timeout callback silences it, but I do not understand why the outer one is not enough, and I would rather not sprinkle `!` everywhere. Is this about `current` being mutable, or about the callback boundary?

A server component renders a formatted date and the browser console throws a hydration error on every load.

```javascript
export default function PostMeta({ createdAt }) {
  return <time dateTime={createdAt}>{new Date(createdAt).toLocaleDateString()}</time>;
}
```

The error says the server rendered `1/5/2026` and the client rendered `05/01/2026`, and React discards the server HTML for that subtree. Next.js 16, node 22, my machine is in Europe/Warsaw and the deploy region is UTC. I tried passing an explicit locale (`toLocaleDateString('en-GB')`) and the day/month order matched, but a colleague in another timezone still gets a mismatch on dates near midnight. What is the actual fix here that does not involve rendering the date only on the client?

After upgrading to Next.js 16 my dynamic route stopped type-checking.

```typescript
export default function QuestionPage({ params }: { params: { id: string } }) {
  return <h1>{params.id}</h1>;
}
```

`next build` fails with: Type '{ params: { id: string; }; }' does not satisfy the constraint 'PageProps<"/questions/[id]">'. Types of property 'params' are incompatible. Type '{ id: string; }' is missing the following properties from type 'Promise<{ id: string; }>': then, catch, finally.

The page worked unchanged on 15. I tried widening the type to `any`, which builds but obviously kills autocomplete and feels like hiding the problem. What is the correct signature now, and does the same change apply to `searchParams` and to `generateMetadata`?

This narrows fine if I access the property on the original object, but not through the rest.

```typescript
type Result =
  | { ok: true; data: string }
  | { ok: false; error: string };

function render(res: Result) {
  const { ok, ...rest } = res;
  if (ok) {
    return rest.data;
    //     ~~~~~~~~~ Property 'data' does not exist on type '{ data: string; } | { error: string; }'
  }
  return rest.error;
}
```

TypeScript 5.9, strict. `const { ok, data } = res` plus a check on `ok` does narrow, so the destructuring itself is not the problem. It looks like the rest object is computed before the check and keeps both branches. Is there a way to keep the rest spread and still get narrowing, or do I have to keep the original variable around and narrow on that?

The badge colours work in dev and are completely unstyled after `npm run build`.

```javascript
function Badge({ tone }) {
  // tone is 'red' | 'green' | 'amber'
  return <span className={`bg-${tone}-100 text-${tone}-700 px-2 py-1`}>{tone}</span>;
}
```

In the built CSS there is no `bg-red-100` at all, only the static `px-2 py-1`. Tailwind v4 with the Vite plugin. I tried adding the files to the source globs and restarting, no change, and I would rather not maintain a `safelist` of every colour and shade. The docs mention that class names have to appear as complete strings but I do not understand how the scanner decides what counts as one. What is the intended pattern for a variant prop like this?

Compilation fails as soon as I use `@apply` outside the entry stylesheet.

```css
/* Card.module.css */
.card {
  @apply rounded-lg border border-gray-200 p-4;
}
```

Error: Cannot apply unknown utility class `rounded-lg`. Are you using CSS modules or similar and missing `@reference`?

The exact same declarations work if I paste them into the file that has `@import "tailwindcss"`. Tailwind v4.1 with the PostCSS plugin, Vite 6. I tried importing `tailwindcss` at the top of the module file, which then duplicated the whole framework CSS into every module. What is the right way to reach the utility definitions from a module file without emitting them again?

The table header is sticky in a bare page and not sticky once I put the table into my layout.

```css
.table-wrapper {
  overflow-x: auto;
}

thead th {
  position: sticky;
  top: 0;
}
```

The wrapper is there because the table is wider than the column on small screens. With the wrapper removed the header pins correctly; with it, the header scrolls away like a normal cell. No `transform` and no `overflow: hidden` anywhere on the ancestors, I checked each one in DevTools. Setting a `height` on the wrapper makes the header stick, but then the page has two scrollbars. Why does a horizontal-only overflow break vertical stickiness?

I am migrating away from the deprecated `@import` and every partial now fails to compile.

```css
/* _variables.scss */
$brand: #c00;

/* _button.scss */
@use '../variables';

.btn { background: $brand; }
```

Error: Undefined variable. `$brand` is undefined at _button.scss line 4.

With `@import '../variables'` this worked for years. Dart Sass 1.83. I read that `@use` is not transitive, so I also added the `@use` line to every partial that needs it, which I did here, and it still fails. Do I have to prefix the variable with the module name, and if so what is the rule for the default namespace?

A mixin that has not changed in two years no longer compiles.

```css
@mixin gutter($gap) {
  padding: $gap / 2;
}

.row { @include gutter(16px); }
```

Error: Undefined operation "16px / 2".

Dart Sass 1.83, previously 1.32 where this only printed a deprecation warning. I tried wrapping it as `($gap / 2)` and `#{$gap / 2}`; the parenthesised version still errors and the interpolated one compiles but returns the literal string `16px/2`, which the browser drops. What is the supported replacement, and does it need an extra `@use` line at the top of the file?

I open a confirmation modal, delete the row, and remove the modal element from the DOM. The grey backdrop stays and the page is dead.

```javascript
const modalEl = document.querySelector('#confirm');
const modal = new bootstrap.Modal(modalEl);
modal.show();

document.querySelector('#confirm-yes').addEventListener('click', () => {
  row.remove();
  modalEl.remove();
});
```

After that, `.modal-backdrop` is still in the body and `<body>` keeps `overflow: hidden` and the `modal-open` class. Bootstrap 5.3.3, bundle build, no jQuery. Removing `.modal-backdrop` by hand in the console unblocks the page, so I could just do that in code, but that feels like fighting the library. What is the correct teardown order when the modal element itself has to go?

The delete button works on the rows rendered by the server and does nothing on rows I append from an AJAX response.

```javascript
$('.row-delete').on('click', function () {
  $(this).closest('tr').remove();
});

$.get('/api/rows', function (html) {
  $('#rows').append(html);
});
```

The appended markup has the same `class="row-delete"`, I confirmed in the inspector. jQuery 3.7.1. Re-running the binding after every append does work, but then old rows end up with the handler attached several times and one click deletes two rows. I assume the selector is evaluated once at bind time. What is the standard way to bind this so it covers future elements without stacking handlers?

The model loads (I can see it in the scene graph and the bounding box is right) but every surface is pure black.

```javascript
const scene = new THREE.Scene();
const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true });

new GLTFLoader().load('/model.glb', (gltf) => {
  scene.add(gltf.scene);
});

renderer.render(scene, camera);
```

The same file looks correct in the online glTF viewer, so the asset is probably fine. If I swap the materials for `MeshBasicMaterial` the geometry shows up with its texture, which tells me the mesh and UVs are okay. The materials in the file are `MeshStandardMaterial`. There are no lights in my scene at all. Is a single `AmbientLight` enough here or does a PBR material need an environment map to look like the reference render?

I rebuild a chart of ~5000 cubes every time the data changes and the tab climbs to 2 GB after a few minutes.

```javascript
function rebuild(points) {
  scene.remove(group);
  group = new THREE.Group();
  for (const p of points) {
    const geo = new THREE.BoxGeometry(1, p.value, 1);
    const mat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: p.color });
    group.add(new THREE.Mesh(geo, mat));
  }
  scene.add(group);
}
```

`renderer.info.memory.geometries` goes 5000, 10000, 15000 and never drops. `scene.children.length` stays at 2, so the objects really are detached. three.js r170. I tried calling `group.clear()` before the reassignment and forcing GC in DevTools; the counter still only grows. What exactly holds on to those buffers after `remove()` and what has to be called per mesh?

The template never updates after the fetch resolves.

```javascript
import { reactive } from 'vue';

export default {
  setup() {
    const state = reactive({ loading: true, items: [] });

    fetch('/api/items')
      .then(r => r.json())
      .then(items => {
        state.items = items;
        state.loading = false;
      });

    const { loading, items } = state;
    return { loading, items };
  },
};
```

The network request succeeds and logging `state.items` inside `then` shows the array, but the template keeps rendering the spinner. Returning `state` itself and using `state.loading` in the template works. Vue 3.5. I get that the destructured `loading` is just a boolean copy, but `items` is an array, so I expected at least that one to stay linked. Why does neither work, and what is the supported way to expose individual fields?

I destructure props for readability and my watcher stopped running.

```typescript
const { userId } = defineProps<{ userId: string }>();

watch(userId, (id) => {
  load(id);
});
```

At runtime I get: [Vue warn] Invalid watch source: undefined. A watch source can only be a getter/effect function, a ref, a reactive object, or an array of these types.

With `const props = defineProps<...>()` and `watch(() => props.userId, ...)` everything is fine. Vue 3.5.13, TypeScript 5.8. The release notes say destructured props stay reactive now, and interpolating `userId` in the template does update, so it clearly is reactive in some sense. Why does `watch` not accept it and what is the correct source expression when I want to keep the destructuring?

The child component exists and is exported, but the parent template refuses to render it.

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-user-list',
  template: `<app-user-card *ngFor="let u of users" [user]="u"></app-user-card>`,
})
export class UserListComponent {
  users = [];
}
```

NG0304: 'app-user-card' is not a known element. 1. If 'app-user-card' is an Angular component, then verify that it is included in the '@Component.imports' of this component.

Angular 19, everything standalone, no NgModules in the project. `UserCardComponent` is declared with `standalone: true` (the default now) and I import the file at the top, but the import is only used by the type annotation. I also get a second error about `*ngFor` on the same template. Do both of those come from the same missing piece?

The count in the template never changes although the handler clearly runs.

```javascript
let store = $state({ count: 0, label: 'clicks' });

let { count, label } = store;

function inc() {
  store.count += 1;
  console.log(store.count); // 1, 2, 3 ...
}
```

The template renders `{count}` and stays at 0 forever, while the log shows the value going up. Svelte 5.20, runes mode, no legacy `export let` in this file. If I render `{store.count}` instead it updates immediately. I assumed `$state` made the whole object reactive so any binding derived from it would follow. Is destructuring taking a snapshot of the proxy, and what should I use when I want short names in the template?

A user picks 2026-03-01 and the row saves as 28 February.

```javascript
const input = document.querySelector('#due');
input.addEventListener('change', () => {
  const d = new Date(input.value); // '2026-03-01'
  console.log(d.toString());
  // Sun Feb 28 2026 ... GMT-0500 (Eastern Standard Time)
  save({ due: d.getDate() });      // 28
});
```

On my machine (UTC+1) it looks correct, which is why it took a week to notice. I understand something timezone-related is happening between the string and the local date, but I do not see where the shift is introduced, since the input value has no time or offset in it at all. I tried appending `T00:00:00` and the number is right, but I want to know why that changes anything before I ship it. Is there a parsing rule that treats the bare date form differently?

After the upgrade, passing a ref to my own component no longer type-checks.

```typescript
type Props = { label: string };

function TextField({ label }: Props) {
  return <input aria-label={label} />;
}

// call site
const ref = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null);
<TextField ref={ref} label="Email" />;
// Property 'ref' does not exist on type 'IntrinsicAttributes & Props'.
```

React 19.2, @types/react 19. I read that `forwardRef` is no longer needed because `ref` is a regular prop, and the runtime does work if I ignore the error with a cast. So the mechanism is there and only the types are missing something. What do I have to add to `Props`, and where does the ref have to be forwarded to inside the component for it to land on the actual `<input>`?

A server component loads a row and hands it to a client child.

```javascript
// page.tsx (server)
const question = await db.question.findFirst({ where: { id } });
return <QuestionCard question={question} />;

// question-card.tsx
'use client';
export function QuestionCard({ question }) {
  return <time>{question.createdAt.toISOString()}</time>;
}
```

Warning: Only plain objects can be passed to Client Components from Server Components. Class instances are not supported.

Next.js 16. `createdAt` is a `Date`, and the row also has a `Decimal` column from the ORM. The page still renders, which confused me at first, but the warning is constant and `toISOString` throws in production. I do not want to serialise the whole row by hand in every page. Is there a rule for which types survive the boundary, and where is the right place to convert?

I wrote a helper that produces dotted paths of an object type and it explodes on our config type.

```typescript
type Path<T> = T extends object
  ? { [K in keyof T & string]: K | `${K}.${Path<T[K]>}` }[keyof T & string]
  : never;

type ConfigPath = Path<AppConfig>;
// error ts(2589): Type instantiation is excessively deep and possibly infinite.
```

It works on small types; `AppConfig` is about six levels deep with a couple of arrays. TypeScript 5.9. Adding a depth counter as a second parameter helps, but I had to cap it at 4 and now the deepest keys are missing. Is there a way to write this so it terminates for arbitrary depth, or is the recursion limit a hard wall that means I should generate the union from the runtime schema instead?

The assertion passes and the test still prints a warning that fails our CI on strict mode.

```javascript
test('shows the user name', async () => {
  render(<Profile id="1" />);
  expect(await screen.findByText('Ada')).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```

Warning: An update to Profile inside a test was not wrapped in act(...).

The component fetches in an effect and calls `setUser` when it resolves; the fetch is mocked to resolve immediately. React 19.2, @testing-library/react 16, Jest 29 with jsdom. `findByText` already wraps in `asyncWrapper`, which is why I expected no warning at all. Wrapping the render in `await act(async () => {...})` removes it, but then I have two nested acts and the intent is unclear. Which update is escaping the wrapper here?

Navigating away from the page leaves the handler running and it throws on unmounted state.

```javascript
useEffect(() => {
  function handleScroll() {
    setOffset(window.scrollY);
  }
  window.addEventListener('scroll', handleScroll);
  return () => window.removeEventListener('scroll', () => handleScroll());
}, []);
```

After a few route changes I can see six scroll listeners in the DevTools event listeners panel and the page gets visibly janky. The cleanup definitely runs, I logged it. React 19.2 with the router doing client-side navigation. I thought passing the same named function was enough. Is the arrow wrapper in the cleanup the problem, and if so why does `removeEventListener` fail silently instead of telling me the listener was not found?

I toggle a class on the root element and no `dark:` utility takes effect.

```css
@import "tailwindcss";
```

```javascript
document.documentElement.classList.toggle('dark', isDark);
```

`<html class="dark">` is there in the inspector, and `dark:bg-gray-900` is present in the generated CSS — but wrapped in `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)`, so it only fires when the OS is dark. Tailwind v4.1. In v3 I set `darkMode: 'class'` in the config, and v4 has no config file in this project. I tried adding the old config back and it is ignored. How do I redefine the `dark` variant so it keys off the class instead of the media query?

I load locale files on demand and the main chunk grew by 400 kB.

```javascript
async function loadLocale(code) {
  const mod = await import(`./locales/${code}.json`);
  return mod.default;
}
```

The build output lists 47 separate chunks, one per file in `locales/`, and the analyzer shows they are all reachable from the entry. Webpack 5. I expected one chunk to be fetched at runtime for the single locale a user needs. If I hardcode `import('./locales/en.json')` I get exactly one chunk as expected, so it is the interpolation. I can build an explicit map of arrow functions, which works but is 47 lines I have to keep in sync. Is there a way to keep the template literal and narrow what gets included?

Dev server runs fine, `vite build` fails on files that import types the normal way.

```typescript
import { Props, formatLabel } from './helpers';

export function Chip({ label }: Props) {
  return <span>{formatLabel(label)}</span>;
}
```

Error: 'Props' is a type and must be imported using a type-only import when 'verbatimModuleSyntax' is enabled.

Vite 6, TypeScript 5.9. `verbatimModuleSyntax` came in with the template's tsconfig and I do not want to turn it off without knowing what it protects. Splitting into `import type { Props }` plus a value import fixes each file, but there are around 200. Is the inline `import { type Props, formatLabel }` form equivalent here, and is there a codemod-safe rule I can turn on in eslint so this does not come back?

Typing in the search box re-renders the whole page including a 500-row table that does not use the query at all.

```javascript
export function AppProvider({ children }) {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);

  return (
    <AppContext.Provider value={{ query, setQuery, user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </AppContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

The table consumer only reads `user`, but the profiler shows it re-rendering on every keystroke. React 19.2. I wrapped the table in `memo`, which changed nothing, and I now understand that memo does not stop a context update from propagating. Wrapping the value in `useMemo` on `[query, user]` still re-renders it whenever `query` changes. Is splitting into two contexts the only real answer here, or is there a way to subscribe to one field?

The template shows the empty state forever even though the data is there.

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-orders',
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush,
  template: `<p *ngIf="!orders.length">No orders</p>`,
})
export class OrdersComponent implements OnInit {
  orders: Order[] = [];

  ngOnInit() {
    this.socket.orders$.subscribe(list => {
      this.orders = list;
      console.log('got', list.length); // logs 12
    });
  }
}
```

Angular 19. It works if I remove `OnPush`, and it also works if I inject `ChangeDetectorRef` and call `markForCheck()` in the subscribe. The stream comes from a raw WebSocket wrapped in a Subject, not from HttpClient. I would like to know why the assignment does not schedule a check under OnPush when an HTTP response in the same component does, and whether the async pipe would remove the need for the manual call.

Opening any protected page freezes the tab and the console fills with router warnings.

```javascript
router.beforeEach((to, from, next) => {
  if (!store.isAuthenticated) {
    next('/login');
  } else {
    next();
  }
});
```

The warning is: Detected an infinite redirection in a navigation guard when going from "/dashboard" to "/login". Aborting to avoid a Stack Overflow.

Vue 3.5, vue-router 4.5. It is obvious in hindsight that `/login` itself hits the guard and gets redirected to `/login` again, but I am not sure what the clean fix is. Checking `to.path !== '/login'` works until we add a second public route. Is a per-route meta flag the intended pattern here, and does returning a location instead of calling `next` behave differently?

Navigating from /questions/1 to /questions/2 keeps showing question 1.

```javascript
// +page.server.js
export async function load({ fetch, url }) {
  const id = url.pathname.split('/').pop();
  const res = await fetch(`/api/questions/${id}`);
  return { question: await res.json() };
}
```

A full page reload shows the right question, so the endpoint is fine. SvelteKit 2.15. I read that load re-runs when a dependency changes, and it looks like reading the id out of `url.pathname` is not registering as a dependency on `params`. Using `params.id` directly does fix it. I would still like to understand the tracking rule, because I have another load that reads `url.searchParams` and I cannot tell whether that one is safe.

The toast is announced the first time and silent for every message after it.

```javascript
function Toast({ message }) {
  if (!message) return null;
  return <div role="status" aria-live="polite">{message}</div>;
}
```

Tested with VoiceOver on Safari 18 and NVDA on Firefox 135; both read the first toast and nothing afterwards. React 19.2. The DOM inspector shows the div being removed and a new one inserted with the next message, which I think is the problem: the live region is created at the same moment as its content instead of existing beforehand. Rendering the wrapper always and only swapping the text inside makes NVDA announce every message, but VoiceOver still skips some. Is there a rule about how long the region must exist before the update?

Reports render fine everywhere except on iPhones, where every timestamp shows as NaN.

```javascript
const raw = '2026-01-05 10:00:00'; // straight from the API
const d = new Date(raw);
console.log(d.getTime());
// Chrome 133: 1767607200000
// Safari 18:  NaN
```

The API is a Python service returning `str(datetime)` with a space between the date and the time. I assumed date parsing was standardised. Replacing the space with `T` makes Safari parse it, but then the value is treated as local time in one browser and I am not sure it is consistent. Which formats is `Date` actually required to accept, and is the fix on my side or should the API emit ISO-8601 with an offset?

The formatting works, editing in the middle does not.

```javascript
function PhoneInput() {
  const [value, setValue] = useState('');

  function onChange(e) {
    const digits = e.target.value.replace(/\D/g, '').slice(0, 9);
    const formatted = digits.replace(/(\d{3})(?=\d)/g, '$1 ');
    setValue(formatted);
  }

  return <input value={value} onChange={onChange} inputMode="tel" />;
}
```

If the field holds `123 456 789` and I click between the 1 and the 2 to fix a typo, the character lands there for a moment and then the caret snaps to the end, so the next keystroke goes to the wrong place. React 19.2, Chrome 133. I understand the value is being replaced wholesale on each render, which resets the selection. What is the correct way to restore the caret when the inserted separators shift the offset?

The build is green and the deployed job crashes on the first call.

```typescript
const byTag = Object.groupBy(questions, (q) => q.tag);
// TypeError: Object.groupBy is not a function
```

TypeScript 5.9 accepts it because `lib` is set to `["ES2024", "DOM"]` in tsconfig, and my local Node is 22 where it works. The container image is `node:20-alpine`, and on Node 20 the method does not exist. So the type layer and the runtime disagree and nothing warned me. I can hand-roll a `reduce` in three lines and probably will, but I would like to stop this class of bug in general: is there a way to make the compiler reject APIs the target runtime does not have, short of guessing the right `lib` value by hand?
